---
import type { TocItem } from '../consts';
import {
  CORE_NON_CLAIMS,
  NOMUE_GLOBAL_INSTALL_COMMAND,
  NOMUE_NPX_COMMAND,
  NOMUE_POSITION,
  NOMUE_VERIFY_COMMAND,
  PAIRED_T_BOUNDARY,
  PAIRED_T_RFC_URL,
  PRODUCT_CAPABILITY,
  PRODUCT_RELEASE_BOUNDARY,
  PUBLIC_CAPABILITY,
  PUBLIC_RELEASE,
} from '../data/site-facts';
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';

const toc: TocItem[] = [
  { id: 'available', label: 'What you can use now' },
  { id: 'what', label: 'What nomue is' },
  { id: 'roles', label: 'Division of roles' },
  { id: 'call', label: 'How a call works' },
  { id: 'availability', label: 'Product and roadmap' },
  { id: 'trust', label: 'Public trust layer' },
  { id: 'agent-readable', label: 'Agent-readable docs' },
  { id: 'limits', label: 'What a result means' },
];
---

<PageLayout
  title="nomue"
  description="nomue is Licklider's scientific verification product for bounded, agent-callable verification work."
  toc={toc}
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">nomue</p>
    <h1>Give research agents a separate statistical verification call</h1>
    <p class="standfirst">
      nomue checks a supported statistical decision, recomputes covered quantities, and
      returns a machine-readable result with its evidence, version, and next action.
    </p>
    <p class="head-links"><a href="/docs/record-verification/">Install and run →</a> · <a href={PUBLIC_RELEASE.npmUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">npm package ↗</a> · <a href={PUBLIC_RELEASE.verifierUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub source ↗</a> · <a href="/docs/examples/">See examples →</a></p>
    <p class="release-facts">Public npm package · <code>{PUBLIC_RELEASE.npmDistTag}</code>: {PUBLIC_RELEASE.verifierPackage} release candidate · Linux / macOS / Windows · Node.js 20 / 22</p>
  </header>

  <section id="available" class="section section--lead">
    <p class="eyebrow">Available now</p>
    <h2>Verify a Release 1 Welch Record locally</h2>
    <p class="capability-lead">{PUBLIC_CAPABILITY}</p>
    <div class="capability-list">
      <article>
        <h3>Check the exact bundle</h3>
        <p>Confirm that the Record uses the registered Release 1 profile, procedure, and version.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Recompute covered quantities</h3>
        <p>Compare registered numerical results under the published verification rule.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Return structured evidence</h3>
        <p>Produce scoped pass, fail, refusal, or unsupported outcomes in a JSON report.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <pre class="quickstart"><code>{NOMUE_GLOBAL_INSTALL_COMMAND}
{NOMUE_VERIFY_COMMAND}

# Or run without a global install
{NOMUE_NPX_COMMAND}</code></pre>
    <p>
      After npm installs the package and its dependencies, Record verification stays on
      the local machine and does not call a nomue server. The package path is exercised
      in CI on Linux, macOS, and Windows with Node.js 20 and 22.
    </p>
    <p>
      For an agent workflow, this is a concrete verification-call boundary: provide a
      versioned Record to a separate command and carry its scoped JSON result back into
      the workflow without asking the research model to judge its own calculation.
    </p>
    <p>
      The same CLI provides <code>canonicalize</code> and <code>digest</code> utilities for
      local Record processing. Their exact arguments and output behavior are published
      before installation in the CLI reference.
    </p>
    <p><a href="/docs/record-verification/">Install and run the verifier →</a> · <a href="/docs/cli-reference/">Read the CLI reference →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section id="what" class="section">
    <h2>What nomue is</h2>
    <p>{NOMUE_POSITION}</p>
    <p>{PRODUCT_CAPABILITY}</p>
    <p>
      The product question is deliberately narrow: given these research conditions and
      this calculation or result, may the result be relied on for this explicitly
      bounded meaning?
    </p>
    <p>
      The result tells the agent what to do next: execute a supported path, ask for one
      missing scientific fact, route an unsupported request elsewhere, preserve a failed
      check, or stop safely.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="roles" class="section">
    <h2>A deliberate division of roles</h2>
    <div class="sequence" aria-label="Research agent, nomue, and researcher roles">
      <div>
        <p class="sequence-label">Research agent</p>
        <p>
          Understand the task, gather context, explain required declarations,
          orchestrate the call, and explain the returned evidence and reason codes.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="sequence-label">nomue</p>
        <p>
          Apply the exact capability and version boundary, protect scientific
          declarations from silent inference, execute the registered deterministic
          path, and return scoped machine-readable outcomes.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="sequence-label">Researcher</p>
        <p>
          Supply scientific facts that cannot be inferred, resolve experimental meaning,
          and remain responsible for the research judgment.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="call" class="section">
    <h2>How a verification call works</h2>
    <ol class="call-steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Identify.</strong> The agent names the exact capability, version, and
        question rather than asking for generic correctness.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Declare.</strong> Required scientific facts are supplied by an authorized
        source or remain explicitly unresolved; the model does not fill them in.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Decide.</strong> nomue determines whether the supported path can proceed,
        needs clarification, or must stop.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Check.</strong> A supported numerical answer comes from deterministic
        execution and the applicable verification contract, not generated prose.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Return the evidence.</strong> The outcome includes the scoped result,
        evidence, versions, next action, and the exact boundary of the check.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p><a href="/docs/verification-call/">Read the verification-call guide →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section id="availability" class="section">
    <h2>Product and roadmap</h2>
    <div class="status-list">
      <article>
        <p class="status-label">Use today</p>
        <h3>Public npm package, Protocol, and local verifier</h3>
        <p>{`Install ${PUBLIC_RELEASE.npmPackage} ${PUBLIC_RELEASE.verifierPackage} from npm and run it locally against the ${PUBLIC_RELEASE.protocol} support target for ${PUBLIC_RELEASE.profile} under the ${PUBLIC_RELEASE.procedure}.`}</p>
        <p><a href="/docs/record-verification/">How to use the public verifier →</a></p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <p class="status-label">Product capability</p>
        <h3>Welch verification for research agents</h3>
        <p>{PRODUCT_CAPABILITY}</p>
        <p>{PRODUCT_RELEASE_BOUNDARY}</p>
        <p><a href="/latest/">Follow Latest →</a> · <a href="/rss.xml">RSS →</a></p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <p class="status-label">Next method</p>
        <h3>Paired-t verification</h3>
        <p>{PAIRED_T_BOUNDARY}</p>
        <p><a href={PAIRED_T_RFC_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Public RFC →</a></p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="trust" class="section">
    <h2>The public trust layer</h2>
    <p>
      Anyone can inspect the public meaning of a supported Record without relying on
      Licklider's product service. The nomue Protocol defines public Record and scoped
      verification semantics, and the open-source verifier checks the covered
      properties locally and offline.
    </p>
    <p>
      The product layer remains separate. It can manage agent-facing intake,
      clarification, execution, and operational behavior without making every product
      implementation choice part of the royalty-free public Protocol.
    </p>
    <p class="links">
      <a href={PUBLIC_RELEASE.protocolUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">nomue Protocol →</a><br />
      <a href={PUBLIC_RELEASE.npmUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">nomue verifier on npm →</a><br />
      <a href={PUBLIC_RELEASE.verifierUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">nomue verifier →</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="agent-readable" class="section">
    <h2>Agent-readable by design</h2>
    <p>
      The documentation states when a verification call applies, when it does not,
      which facts must not be inferred, the possible non-success outcomes, and the
      limits of a successful result. Clean Markdown pages and machine-readable examples
      expose the same underlying boundary to agents and researchers.
    </p>
    <p>
      This documentation helps an agent select a capability, supply the right facts, and
      interpret the returned outcome. The versioned runtime schema and public Protocol
      remain the exact execution and verification contracts.
    </p>
    <p><a href="/docs/">Agent-readable documentation →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section id="limits" class="section">
    <h2>What a successful nomue result means</h2>
    <p>
      A successful result means that the named checks passed under the stated capability,
      procedure, evidence, and version. It gives the research agent and researcher a
      checkable basis for that bounded decision.
    </p>
    <p>The following questions remain with the researcher or another capability:</p>
    <ul>
      {CORE_NON_CLAIMS.map((claim) => <li>{claim};</li>)}
    </ul>
    <p>
      Keeping these questions separate makes the positive result useful: each passing
      check says exactly what can be relied on and why.
    </p>
  </section>

  <footer class="page-foot">
    <p>Available now: local verification of supported Release 1 Welch Records.</p>
    <p><a href="/docs/record-verification/">Run the verifier →</a></p>
  </footer>
</PageLayout>

<style>
  .page-head { padding-bottom: 2.5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .page-head .eyebrow { margin: 0; }
  .page-head h1 { margin: 0.9rem 0 1.25rem; font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 3.6vw, 2.6rem); line-height: 1.25; letter-spacing: -0.01em; }
  .standfirst { margin: 0; font-size: 1.08rem; color: var(--ink-2); }
  .head-links { margin: 1.75rem 0 0; font-size: 0.92rem; }
  .release-facts { margin: 0.8rem 0 0; color: var(--ink-3); font-size: 0.78rem; }
  .section { padding: 2.75rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule-faint); }
  .section h2 { margin-bottom: 1rem; font-size: 1.25rem; }
  .section > :last-child, article > :last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .section--lead h2 { max-width: 38rem; font-size: clamp(1.55rem, 3vw, 2.1rem); line-height: 1.25; }
  .capability-lead { max-width: 46rem; color: var(--ink-1); font-size: 1.08rem; }
  .capability-list { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; margin: 1.75rem 0; }
  .capability-list article { padding: 1.2rem 1.25rem; border: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .capability-list h3 { margin-bottom: 0.65rem; font-size: 1rem; }
  .quickstart { margin: 1.75rem 0; overflow-x: auto; padding: 1.1rem 1.2rem; background: var(--wash); border: 1px solid var(--rule); font-size: 0.78rem; line-height: 1.65; }
  .quickstart code { font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, monospace; }
  .sequence, .status-list { display: grid; gap: 1rem; margin-top: 1.75rem; }
  .sequence > div { padding-left: 1.25rem; border-left: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .sequence-label, .status-label { margin-bottom: 0.35rem; color: var(--ink-3); font-size: 0.75rem; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .call-steps { margin-top: 1.5rem; }
  strong { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 400; }
  .status-list article { padding: 1.25rem; border: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .status-list h3 { margin-bottom: 0.65rem; font-size: 1.05rem; }
  .links { margin-top: 1.75rem; }
  .page-foot { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 1.5rem; flex-wrap: wrap; padding: 2rem 0 0; font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--ink-3); }
  .page-foot p { margin: 0; }

  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .capability-list { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  }
</style>
